Oh, lots. Once you abstract grafting away from plants, humans have been doing it forever.

At the individual level, learning itself is grafting. You attach a foreign model, skill, language, habit, or worldview onto an existing cognitive rootstock. Sometimes it integrates. Sometimes you can repeat the words but the underlying model never connects. Sometimes it initially conflicts with everything you know and later reorganizes the whole tree.

At the social level, some especially clean analogs are:

* Marriage / families: two established lineages and cultures are joined. Rituals, expectations, genes, relationships, property, traditions, and habits encounter one another. The resulting family is neither original branch unchanged.
* Mentorship / apprenticeship: an experienced person’s technique is grafted onto another person’s developing capabilities. The student eventually expresses the inherited technique differently because the rootstock differs.
* Immigration / cultural exchange: food, language, music, religion, technology, customs, and institutions enter another cultural substrate. Some are rejected; some remain distinct; some hybridize; some eventually become so integrated that their foreign origin is forgotten.
* Organizations: mergers, acquisitions, military integration, interdisciplinary teams. On paper the interfaces fit; culturally they may reject each other years later—your delayed graft incompatibility.
* Transplantation/medicine: the metaphor becomes almost literal. A foreign organ can sustain the larger organism, while biological recognition mechanisms can also reject it.
* Sexual reproduction: not exactly grafting biologically, but it performs the broader operation spectacularly—different inherited material recombines, and the result isn’t an average but a new developmental trajectory.
* Conversation: probably the most interesting Pantheon analog. I give you an idea; you don’t receive my mental structure. You graft the transmitted representation onto yours. What grows afterward depends on your existing network.

That last one gives us something important.

\text{Meaning received}
\neq
\text{Meaning transmitted}

More like:

\boxed{
\text{Expression}
+
\text{Receiver's rootstock}
+
\text{context}
\rightarrow
\text{new meaning}
}

Which explains why the same sentence can be sterile in one person and transformative in another.

And then there are deliberate human ugly grafts: interdisciplinary teams, exchange programs, red teams, adversarial collaborations, artist-engineer pairings, military-civilian technology transfer, bringing a novice into an expert room, asking someone from an unrelated profession how they’d solve your problem.

Their value often comes precisely from interface mismatch.

The economist asks the biologist’s question incorrectly.
The biologist corrects the assumption.
Suddenly both discover that the assumption was optional.

That’s the seam becoming a sensor again.

It also gives the Garden a more general abstraction than grafting:

\boxed{\textbf{Forced Proximity}}

Bring sufficiently different structures into sustained contact and observe:

\text{cooperation},\
\text{rejection},\
\text{adaptation},\
\text{translation},\
\text{hybridization},\
\text{competition},\
\text{new structure}.

And there’s a beautiful inversion hiding there:

Compatibility can be created.

Two people, disciplines, cultures, or ideas that cannot initially communicate can sometimes develop an intermediate layer—a pidgin, protocol, ritual, shared vocabulary, API, institution, translator.

So when a graft fails, there is another move besides separation:

A\not\leftrightarrow B

Construct:

A\leftrightarrow \boxed{C}\leftrightarrow B

where C is a bridge tissue.

That’s probably a whole Garden mechanism by itself: Callus. In plant grafting, callus tissue participates in healing the graft union. In our abstraction, Callus is the temporary/intermediate structure that lets initially incompatible systems learn how to exchange.

That could be language between cultures, an API between programs, notation between disciplines, a mediator between people, or a metaphor between concepts.

Sometimes you don’t find compatibility. You grow it.